  "nl_text": "The database schema \"schemabench_0148\" consists of 8 tables. The main entities in this schema are Orders, Payments, Refunds, Order Items, Products, Categories, Users, and Invoices.\nThe Orders table represents a collection of orders made by users. It has an id as the primary key, user_id as a foreign key referencing the Users table, total_amount as a numeric value representing the total amount of the order, status as a string representing the status of the order, and timestamps for created_at and updated_at.\nThe Payments table represents the payments made for the orders. It has an id as the primary key, invoice_id as a foreign key referencing the Invoices table, amount as a numeric value representing the payment amount, payment_date as a timestamp representing the date of payment, method as a string representing the payment method, and timestamps for created_at and updated_at.\nThe Refunds table represents the refunds made for the orders. It has an id as the primary key, order_id as a foreign key referencing the Orders table, amount as a numeric value representing the refund amount, reason as a text field representing the reason for the refund, refunded_at as a timestamp representing the date of refund, and timestamps for created_at and updated_at.\nThe Order Items table represents the items in the orders. It has an id as the primary key, order_id and product_id as foreign keys referencing the Orders and Products tables respectively, quantity as an integer representing the quantity of the product in the order, price as a numeric value representing the price of the product, and timestamps for created_at and updated_at.\nThe Products table represents the products available for purchase. It has an id as the primary key, name as a string representing the name of the product, category_id as a foreign key referencing the Categories table, price as a numeric value representing the price of the product, stock as an integer representing the stock of the product, description as a text field representing the description of the product, and timestamps for created_at and updated_at.\nThe Categories table represents the categories of the products. It has an id as the primary key, name as a string representing the name of the category, description as a text field representing the description of the category, and timestamps for created_at and updated_at.\nThe Users table represents the users of the system. It has an id as the primary key, name as a string representing the name of the user, email as a string representing the email of the user, email_verified_at as a timestamp representing the date of email verification, password as a string representing the password of the user, remember_token as a string representing the remember token of the user, and timestamps for created_at and updated_at.\nThe Invoices table represents the invoices for the orders. It has an id as the primary key, order_id as a foreign key referencing the Orders table, amount as a numeric value representing the invoice amount, issued_date as a timestamp representing the date of invoice issuance, due_date as a timestamp representing the due date of the invoice, status as a string representing the status of the invoice, and timestamps for created_at and updated_at.\nAll tables allow null values except for the NOT NULL constraints. Default values are set for created_at and updated_at timestamps. The primary keys are id for all tables and foreign keys are used to maintain referential integrity between tables.",
  "gold_question": "Online Accounting Software for Small Businesses and Freelancers\n\n1) Background / Context\n\n   Akaunting is an online accounting software solution tailored for small businesses and freelancers. It leverages modern technologies including Laravel, VueJS, Tailwind, and RESTful APIs. The software features a modular structure that supports an App Store, catering to both users and developers for expanded functionality.\n\n2) Objectives\n\n   - To provide a comprehensive accounting platform that enables small businesses and freelancers to manage financial operations efficiently.\n   - To offer a scalable and customizable solution through an integrated App Store.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n   - Stakeholders: Small business owners, freelancers, developers, and the Akaunting management team.\n   - Users: End-users including small business owners and freelancers who require accounting services, and developers looking to enhance the platform’s functionalities.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n   - User Management: Allow users to register, manage their profile details, and authenticate securely.\n   - Order Management: Users can create and manage orders with associated items, track order statuses, and compute total amounts.\n   - Product Management: Facilitate the management of products, including adding, updating product details, monitoring stock levels, and categorizing products.\n   - Invoice Processing: Generate and manage invoices linked to orders, with capabilities to track due dates and payment statuses.\n   - Payment and Refund Handling: Process payments and log refunds associated with invoices and orders.\n   - Financial Reporting: Generate reports and summaries of sales, payments, and overall financial performance.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n   - Performance: The system must handle multiple concurrent users with efficient response times.\n   - Security: Ensure data privacy and secure handling of sensitive financial information and user credentials.\n   - Scalability: Provide a modular architecture to facilitate easy integration of new features via the App Store.\n   - Usability: Design a user-friendly interface with intuitive navigation and clear access to all major functionalities.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0148.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0148\nCREATE TABLE `orders` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `total_amount` decimal(10,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  KEY `user_id` (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `orders_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `payments` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `invoice_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `payment_date` timestamp DEFAULT NULL,\n  `method` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  KEY `invoice_id` (`invoice_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `payments_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`invoice_id`) REFERENCES `invoices` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `refunds` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `order_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `reason` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `refunded_at` timestamp DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  KEY `order_id` (`order_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `refunds_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`order_id`) REFERENCES `orders` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `order_items` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `order_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `product_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `quantity` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `price` decimal(10,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  KEY `order_id` (`order_id`),\n  KEY `product_id` (`product_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `order_items_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`order_id`) REFERENCES `orders` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `order_items_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`product_id`) REFERENCES `products` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `products` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `category_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `price` decimal(10,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `stock` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  KEY `category_id` (`category_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `products_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`category_id`) REFERENCES `categories` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `categories` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `email_verified_at` timestamp DEFAULT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `remember_token` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `invoices` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `order_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `amount` decimal(10,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `issued_date` timestamp DEFAULT NULL,\n  `due_date` timestamp DEFAULT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  KEY `order_id` (`order_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `invoices_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`order_id`) REFERENCES `orders` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
